<h2>How to Choose</h2>
<p><strong>If you speak on camera already:</strong> start with CapCut (free) plus Descript if your videos are talk-heavy, and add ElevenLabs only for pickups and dubbing. That stack costs $0-16/month.</p>
<p><strong>If you never want to appear on camera:</strong> Synthesia for the presenter and ElevenLabs if you want a different narration voice than the avatar's; both free tiers are enough to produce a pilot before you pay anything.</p>
<p><strong>If your problem is output volume:</strong> the bottleneck is shorts. Submagic if you want captions and clips in one app, OpusClip if you have a large back catalogue and want the virality score to sort it.</p>
<p><strong>If you publish in several languages:</strong> ElevenLabs for voice and Synthesia for avatar-led localisation let one script ship to a dozen markets without reshooting.</p>
<p><strong>Before you subscribe to anything:</strong> convert the plan's credits into finished minutes using the vendor's own conversion table. That single step prevents most of the "cheap plan, expensive month" surprises in this category.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>What is the best AI voiceover tool for YouTube videos?</h3>
<p>ElevenLabs is the most widely used option. Free includes 10,000 credits a month, Starter is $6/month for 30,000 credits and Creator is $22/month for 121,000 credits with professional voice cloning. At roughly one credit per character, 30,000 credits is about 30 minutes of finished speech, so weekly publishers usually land on Creator.</p>

<h3>Can I run a YouTube channel without showing my face?</h3>
<p>Yes. The standard stack is an avatar tool (Synthesia or HeyGen) for the presenter, a voice tool for narration and a clipper for shorts. Synthesia's free Basic plan allows about 10 minutes of video a month with a watermark; Starter is $19/month for 1,250 credits, which the vendor converts to roughly 12 minutes of avatar footage.</p>

<h3>How do I turn long videos into Shorts automatically?</h3>
<p>Submagic and OpusClip both do it. OpusClip's free plan posts one clip a day with a caption watermark and Starter at $15/month removes it. Submagic's Starter is $19/month ($12 yearly) for 45 credits, which converts to about 15 captioned videos or 5 Magic Clips — clips cost more credits than plain captions.</p>

<h3>Is CapCut still free?</h3>
<p>Yes, CapCut keeps a free tier with the timeline editor, templates and auto-captions. Pro, Ultra and Teams sit on top of it, priced in local currency and varying by region and platform; the vendor states displayed prices are for reference until you sign in.</p>

<h3>What should an AI stack cost per month?</h3>
<p>A lean stack in 2026 runs about $30-50: roughly $6-22 for voice, $15-19 for shorts, and $0-10 for editing and thumbnails. The variable that breaks budgets is credit-based pricing for video — always convert credits to finished minutes first.</p>

